Proposed xv6-RISC-V Kernel Enhancements

Project Overview

This project proposes ten enhancements to the existing xv6-RISC-V educational operating system. The aim is to improve process scheduling, memory monitoring, security, resource management, fault diagnosis, and kernel testing.

The enhancements will be studied and developed incrementally. Each module will be tested in the xv6-RISC-V environment to evaluate its correctness and impact on existing kernel functionality.

Objectives

* Improve process and resource management.
* Introduce additional kernel-level security controls.
* Make memory and system-call behavior easier to analyze.
* Improve fault diagnosis and process-failure handling.
* Provide repeatable tests for selected kernel components.

Proposed Modules

1. Adaptive Process Scheduler

Purpose: Improve CPU scheduling by considering process waiting time and CPU usage.

Proposed features:

* Track how long a process waits for CPU time.
* Adjust scheduling priority according to a defined policy.
* Reduce the risk of processes waiting indefinitely.
* Record scheduling statistics for evaluation.

Expected outcome: A scheduling policy that can be compared with the existing xv6 scheduler.

2. Memory Allocation Tracker

Purpose: Monitor memory allocation and release activity in the kernel.

Proposed features:

* Count memory allocation and deallocation events.
* Track allocated and free physical pages.
* Report memory usage statistics.
* Identify suspicious allocation patterns for debugging.

Expected outcome: Better visibility into memory usage and allocation behavior.

3. System Call Permission Guard

Purpose: Introduce configurable restrictions on selected system calls.

Proposed features:

* Define a policy for allowing or blocking selected system calls.
* Apply restrictions to designated processes.
* Record blocked system-call attempts.
* Test whether restrictions are enforced by the kernel.

Expected outcome: A basic kernel-level mechanism for experimenting with system-call access control.

4. Kernel Fault Diagnosis Module

Purpose: Make selected kernel errors easier to investigate.

Proposed features:

* Record important diagnostic events.
* Include relevant process IDs and error information.
* Maintain a limited history of recent events.
* Provide a command to display diagnostic records.

Expected outcome: More useful information for debugging kernel behavior.

5. Resource Quota Manager

Purpose: Apply configurable resource limits to processes.

Proposed features:

* Define a supported resource limit for a process.
* Monitor resource consumption against that limit.
* Handle quota violations according to a documented policy.
* Report processes that reach their limits.

Expected outcome: A basic mechanism for testing controlled resource consumption.

6. File Integrity Verification

Purpose: Detect unexpected changes to selected files.

Proposed features:

* Calculate a checksum for a selected file.
* Store or provide a reference checksum for comparison.
* Verify a file against its reference checksum.
* Report mismatches and verification errors.

Expected outcome: A basic file-change detection mechanism, subject to xv6 filesystem capabilities.

7. Process Failure Monitoring and Recovery

Purpose: Improve visibility into process failures and explore controlled recovery.

Proposed features:

* Record process termination and available exit information.
* Maintain statistics about failed or terminated processes.
* Identify repeated failures in test scenarios.
* Explore restarting a designated user-level test program through a supervisor.

Expected outcome: Better process-failure diagnostics and a foundation for controlled recovery experiments.

8. System Call Performance Analyzer

Purpose: Analyze system-call frequency and performance.

Proposed features:

* Count calls by system-call type.
* Measure elapsed timer ticks where supported.
* Identify frequently used or comparatively expensive calls.
* Display collected statistics through a user-level command.

Expected outcome: Quantitative information for evaluating system-call behavior.

9. Process Sleep and Wake Monitor

Purpose: Study process waiting and wake-up behavior.

Proposed features:

* Record selected sleep and wake-up events.
* Track waiting and wake-up statistics.
* Identify repeated wake-ups in test programs.
* Display collected information for analysis.

Expected outcome: Better understanding of process synchronization and waiting behavior.

10. Kernel Self-Test and Health Monitor

Purpose: Provide repeatable tests for selected kernel functionality.

Proposed features:

* Test memory allocation and release behavior.
* Test process creation and termination.
* Check selected system-call results.
* Report test outcomes and diagnostic information.

Expected outcome: A repeatable testing mechanism to help detect regressions after kernel changes.

Proposed Development Approach

1. Study the relevant xv6-RISC-V source code and documentation.
2. Review the feasibility and scope of each proposed module.
3. Implement changes incrementally, beginning with manageable modules.
4. Test each enhancement in the xv6-RISC-V environment.
5. Check that existing kernel functionality continues to work.
6. Document implementation decisions, limitations, and test results.

Tools and Environment

* xv6-RISC-V source code
* C and RISC-V assembly, where required
* RISC-V toolchain and QEMU emulator
* Git and GitHub for version control
* xv6-RISC-V book and documentation

Project Status

Current stage: Proposal and planning.

The modules listed above are proposed enhancements. Their implementation and testing will be documented as development progresses. Final scope may be refined after feasibility analysis and supervisor feedback.

References

* xv6-RISC-V source code: https://github.com/mit-pdos/xv6-riscv
* xv6-RISC-V book: https://github.com/mit-pdos/xv6-riscv-book









